WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.393
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.393 --> 00:00:06.679
This is the UK Landlord M T D-Ready Bookkeeping Ledger, version one point zero point one.

3
00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:16.107
It is a local-first Microsoft Excel workbook for organising property income and expenses, checking the records, and preparing cumulative figures for compatible bridging software.

4
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:22.000
It does not submit directly to H M R C or calculate your tax bill.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.520
The workbook follows one clear routine.

6
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:34.500
Start here, complete Setup, record transactions, correct any warnings, and then review the summaries and M T D Export.

7
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.020
White cells are for your input.

8
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.700
Protected areas contain formulas.

9
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:45.000
The aim is to keep every reported figure traceable to the transaction that created it.

10
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.192
On Setup, choose the tax year, Standard or Calendar update periods, and Simple or Full reporting detail.

11
00:00:54.192 --> 00:01:00.538
Then give each property a clear nickname and leave properties you currently use marked Active.

12
00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:05.615
For jointly owned property, enter transaction amounts that already represent your share.

13
00:01:05.615 --> 00:01:09.000
The ownership percentage does not adjust figures automatically.

14
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.018
Simple and Full modes control the categories available in the transaction dropdown.

15
00:01:16.018 --> 00:01:20.618
Simple Mode uses a smaller group of income and expense categories.

16
00:01:20.618 --> 00:01:23.545
Full Mode provides more detailed reporting categories.

17
00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:26.473
Select the approach appropriate to your circumstances.

18
00:01:26.473 --> 00:01:34.000
The workbook provides the structure, but it does not decide whether a reporting option is suitable for you.

19
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.940
Your update-period choice controls the valid dates.

20
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.980
Standard periods begin on the sixth of April, July, October and January.

21
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.020
Calendar periods begin on the first of April, July, October and January.

22
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:57.000
Once selected, the workbook validates each transaction date and assigns it to the correct one of the four periods.

23
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.379
Use Transactions for the source records.

24
00:02:01.379 --> 00:02:08.914
Enter the date, an active property, Income or Expense, a permitted category, a useful description, and a positive amount.

25
00:02:08.914 --> 00:02:14.069
Add a receipt reference when it helps connect the row to supporting evidence.

26
00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:18.828
Expenses are entered as positive values and are kept separate from income.

27
00:02:18.828 --> 00:02:22.000
Do not type over the protected system columns.

28
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.600
Warnings tell you that a source row needs attention.

29
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:34.800
Missing amounts, dates outside the selected range, inactive properties, or invalid types and categories can block export readiness.

30
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.800
Return to the original row, correct it, and check again.

31
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:46.000
Duplicate receipt references are review warnings, but they do not by themselves prevent the export from becoming ready.

32
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:55.846
The quarterly summary shows standalone activity for each period, including income, expenses, transaction counts and invalid records.

33
00:02:55.846 --> 00:03:02.308
The annual summary gives a full-year bookkeeping overview, together with category and property totals.

34
00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:06.462
Use both pages to spot values that look unexpected.

35
00:03:06.462 --> 00:03:12.000
These are bookkeeping checks, not calculations of taxable profit or tax due.

36
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.966
M T D Export presents cumulative figures.

37
00:03:16.966 --> 00:03:19.085
Update one contains period one.

38
00:03:19.085 --> 00:03:22.051
Update two includes periods one and two.

39
00:03:22.051 --> 00:03:28.831
Update three includes the first three periods, and update four covers the full selected update year.

40
00:03:28.831 --> 00:03:37.305
Confirm that readiness shows ready, review the setup and totals, then follow the process provided by your compatible bridging software.

41
00:03:37.305 --> 00:03:39.000
Keep a backup first.

42
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.574
Version one supports one thousand preformatted transaction rows.

43
00:03:44.574 --> 00:03:47.702
Automatic expansion beyond that is not supported.

44
00:03:47.702 --> 00:03:54.404
Version one point zero point one has been tested in Microsoft 365 Excel for Windows.

45
00:03:54.404 --> 00:04:02.000
Compatibility with Excel for Mac and Excel 2021 is not claimed until those environments have been tested.

46
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.680
The workbook is local-first.

47
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:14.080
It contains no macros, telemetry, analytics, hidden web requests or built-in cloud synchronisation, and no monthly software account is required.

48
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:25.000
You decide where the file is stored, how it is backed up, and when information is transmitted to another service, including cloud storage or bridging software.

49
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.091
That is the complete workflow.

50
00:04:29.091 --> 00:04:37.036
Set it up, record transactions consistently, correct warnings, review the summaries, and check M T D Export before bridging.

51
00:04:37.036 --> 00:04:42.891
Keep within the documented one thousand rows and use Microsoft 365 Excel for Windows.

52
00:04:42.891 --> 00:04:50.000
The result is a calmer bookkeeping process with clear boundaries and figures that remain easy to trace.

